 "description": "<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:normal;\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:11.5pt;\"> 8vo. f. [1] (blank), pp. [8], 48, f. [1] (blank). Boards. Includes head- and tailpieces and initials.</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:normal;\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:11.5pt;\"><br /></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:normal;\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:11.5pt;\">First edition. A reissue is dated 1715. A poem \u2018written in imitation of Shakespeare\u2019 by the minor playwright, poet, and critic Lewis Theobald, who is otherwise best known for \u2018Double Falsehood, or the Distrest Lovers\u2019 (1728, see Bib# 4103197/Fr# 712 in this collection), a play he claimed to have \u2018revised and adapted to the stage\u2019 from a manuscript version \u2018written originally by W. Shakespeare.\u2019 See also ESTC, T136609; D.F. Foxon, English Verse, 1701-1750, T137.</span><br /></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:normal;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.5pt;font-family:Helvetica, sans-serif;\"><br /></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:normal;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.5pt;font-family:Helvetica, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size:11.5pt;font-family:Helvetica, sans-serif;\"><a href=\"https://catalyst.library.jhu.edu/permalink/01JHU_INST/1lu78g9/alma991039120749707861\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\"></a></span></span></p>",
